+    or single-line file.
+
+    `rewrite` echoes the untouched lines back verbatim, so a new line has to
+    use the same terminator as the line it follows; otherwise a CRLF file ends
+    up with one LF line in the middle of it. The terminator of the *last*
+    terminated line is used rather than the first one, so a file whose lines
+    were written by different tools keeps the convention of the line the new
+    entry is appended to.
+    """
+    if text.endswith("\r\n"):
+        return "\r\n"
+    if text.endswith("\n"):
+        return "\n"
+    if text.endswith("\r"):
+        return "\r"
